WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.750
JASON CLARK: Good afternoon.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.270
This session is how to
industrialize your data

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.360
platform with Google Cloud
Platform and open source

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.650
technology.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:16.880
I'm Jason Clark.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.870
I've been with Monsanto/Bayer
for a little over nine years

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.340
now.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.820
So to start with,
who here has seen

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.800
it an ear of corn up
close and personal?

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.170
If you have, you've
probably noticed

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.420
that on that
individual ear of corn

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.150
there are a lot of
individual kernels, right?

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.930
On an ear of corn, you can have
anywhere between 100 and 1,200

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.620
kernels on that single ear.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.650
Each of those kernels has a lot
of information packed inside.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.230
A lot of information
that's very useful to us

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.870
within Bayer Crop Science
and making decisions

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.450
on how we drive our
product pipeline.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.370
And what I want to
talk to you today

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.880
is essentially asking
the question like,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.430
what if we could take all
that data that's packed

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.318
inside each of those kernels--

00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:10.360
each of those kernels that
are on those ears that

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.890
are in these fields
where there's

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.540
thousands of corn plants.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.360
What if we could take data
about each of those kernels

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.940
and engineer an information
pipeline that can help us mass

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.000
produce knowledge about
our product pipeline

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.420
and ultimately make more
informed decisions that help

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.150
drive useful products to market
that can aid in producing

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.855
more bountiful harvests?

00:01:36.855 --> 00:01:38.230
What I want to
convince you of is

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:41.340
that we have, as an
engineering team,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.450
produced an information
pipeline that

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.100
leverages historic observations,
crop data that we have

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.530
to further characterize
our products

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.230
and ultimately improve
decision making,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.200
but at a fraction of the
costs that we would incur

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.580
to have done it previously.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.770
So let's take a step back here.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.789
I'm with Barry G. I'm actually
more a part of Bayer Crop

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.169
Science.

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:14.550
And when we look at our
product pipeline for Bayer Crop

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:19.530
Science, it really comes down
to three high level steps.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.500
Number one, we're going
to try to identify

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.910
market needs, right?

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.220
What treatments can
we pair with seeds

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.900
and genetic characteristics that
we know about our corn plants?

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.770
As an example, how can we
pair these things together

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:37.520
and produce a product
that ultimately improves

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.900
yield for our farmer customers?

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.620
Once we've identified what
those market needs are,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.410
we'll start to actually
develop those product concepts.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.490
Step two.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.670
When we're developing those,
what we're ultimately doing

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.720
is over the course of years,
taking each one of those seeds,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.080
planting them, pollinating them,
harvesting them, and starting

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.070
that whole cycle over.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:06.150
Shipping seed around the globe
and perform that cycle year

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.770
over year.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:12.630
And while we're planting
and harvesting these things,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.790
in between there, we're
measuring a lot of data

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:19.360
and recording a lot of
data about those seeds.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.220
Fundamentally,
there are two data

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:26.070
sets that our team cares about
that contribute to driving

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.680
that product pipeline.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.960
Genetic data,
which would include

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.390
genomics, and phenotypic data.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.390
So genetic data
being, what do we

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.080
know about the genetic makeup,
the genome of these seeds

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.400
that we're moving through
a product pipeline?

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.700
And phenotypic data being,
what can we actually observe?

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.040
Is this year of corn
yellow, or is it white?

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:48.990
Right?

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.210
That's a very simple
characteristic.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.610
But you can imagine much
more complex characteristics

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.800
that we're measuring as well.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.160
And then ultimately,
once we've identified

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.040
that product concept, we've
developed this product concept,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.190
we're sure it's the right
fit for our customers,

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:06.720
we'll commercialize it.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.403
At each of these
steps along the way,

00:04:08.403 --> 00:04:10.320
there are different
elements of our data stack

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.940
that are important.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.030
And what I want to
do today is tell you

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.550
who we are as an
engineering team,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.570
and then tell you how we've
applied some principles

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:25.680
to industrialize our platform
and allow for large scale

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.380
across our consumers and within
our platform to ultimately

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.270
produce knowledge.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.140
So who are we?

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.750
How do I fit into this picture?

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.850
I work for a really
strong engineering

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:43.360
team called Product360
within our data landscape.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.990
What we do is we engineer a
versatile enterprise wide data

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.980
asset platform that's really
core to other business layers.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:51.980
What does that mean?

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.990
Well, fundamentally, what we
do is we provide access to core

00:04:55.990 --> 00:05:01.780
product data sets in various
ways, various modes of access

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.210
across various personas.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.960
So everything from
other software products

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.000
that are being built
within the company

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.200
to data scientists that
are trying to identify what

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.090
those next product
concepts could be,

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.730
all the way up to our domain
experts, the people working

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:17.540
in the labs.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.460
The people that are
really doing the hands on

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.970
work to measure and
observe characteristics

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:27.500
about these seeds that are
moving through our pipeline.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:33.190
Now what that means is that
with that variety of consumers

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.470
and spanning that long
of a pipeline-- again,

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:37.090
the course of years--

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.250
we really build up
some demand in terms

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.562
of usage of the
platform that we built.

00:05:42.562 --> 00:05:44.395
To kind of give you a
sense for that usage--

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.390
API requests.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.890
So again, the fundamental
mode of access for the data

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.697
sets that our team
delivers is through APIs.

00:05:54.697 --> 00:05:57.030
And we'll get into kind of
the different modes of access

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.930
for those APIs in
just a few moments.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.240
But what you can see here
is on average, monthly,

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.000
over the course
of the last year,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.100
we've had 323
million API requests.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.260
So we're not talking
internet scale.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.623
But we're talking
pretty sizable usage

00:06:16.623 --> 00:06:18.540
when you consider that
all these consumers are

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.590
within our company.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:25.130
Now, another thing that you
can see here is peak usage.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:28.010
And what you can see is that
agriculture is fundamentally

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.630
a seasonal business, right?

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:32.450
We'll see spikes in
usage at various times

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:33.810
throughout the year.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:39.140
We've seen one month where
we have had 1.16 billion API

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.890
requests across our platform.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:43.330
So who are those coming from?

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.830
Well, we measure that as well.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.920
And we'll talk a little
bit later on about how

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.920
we measure this data.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.000
Active applications.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.015
So application being considered.

00:06:55.015 --> 00:06:58.630
A software product that's
using our platform in some way.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:02.170
An analytical model that's
using our platform in some way.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.280
Also, inclusive of
individual data scientists

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.550
that may be using our
platform in some way.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:11.200
We've seen an average of 85
active applications per month,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:17.810
spiking up to, again, 116
with those seasonal workloads.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.630
Now our platform
is not stagnant.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:20.370
It's not stale.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.950
It's changing every day.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.740
We release on average
23 times a month

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.080
to production, with a
peak in there of releasing

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.220
more than one time of day.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.280
Now, we're not releasing
hundreds of times a day.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.100
We don't have that need.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.070
But what it does show is that
our platform is still evolving.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.190
And some of the principles
that I'm going to talk to you

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:48.150
about give us that
wiggle room to continue

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.920
to evolve the platform as we
continue to expand the usage

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.140
and provide value
for our consumers.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.050
Produce new knowledge
for the organization.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.780
To kind of give you a sense of
the breadth of the platform,

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.160
we're looking at a little
over 40 resource collections.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.650
All those data sets,
those resources

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.950
are all different
shapes and sizes.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.580
Some may be graphed
structured data.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:15.432
Some may be traditional
relational data.

00:08:15.432 --> 00:08:16.890
They're all different,
but they all

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.780
fit into the puzzle in some way.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.120
And we don't just
distribute our databases.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.250
We distribute our
engineering team as well.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:30.700
We've got a team of 30-plus
data engineers split into five

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.570
to seven person teams focused
on different vertical areas

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.890
within Product360.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:41.730
So with demand like
that, how do we

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:44.280
go about building
a platform that we

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.460
can scale, that we can use,
that our consumers can use,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.130
and our consumers can scale?

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:54.220
To do that, we've had to
develop some basic principles.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.540
And if we think
about this like we're

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.580
designing a factory
of knowledge here

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.943
and we're trying to layout
our factory floor, what

00:09:01.943 --> 00:09:04.110
are some of the principles
in designing that factory

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.685
floor that we've
tried to follow?

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:12.060
Well, step number one is we want
to minimize retooling, right?

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:15.420
We don't want to have to
reconfigure that factory floor

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.010
and incur downtime in that
production of knowledge

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:20.850
if we don't have to.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.490
We want to minimize that.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.650
Number two is we
want standardization.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:29.650
And we want each component
on that factory floor,

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:32.970
we want its job and its purpose
in life to be obvious, right?

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.730
We want somebody to be able to
look at that layout and say,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.720
I know why this piece is here.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.650
But we also want
that indirection

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.840
as an engineering team
to be able to swap

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.770
a new component in that
it does that job at least

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:50.110
a little bit better
if necessary, right?

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.120
So we want that
ability to refactor,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.790
but we don't want to cause our
consumers to have to retool.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:06.080
The next thing is, if our
factory floor stops working,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:07.520
we're no good for our consumers.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.180
We're not producing knowledge.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.470
We need a really clear view
of the health of our factory

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:13.460
floor, right?

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.710
We need key
performance indicators

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.763
that can tell us how that
factory floor is operating

00:10:18.763 --> 00:10:20.430
and where the bottlenecks
are at, right?

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:21.930
Where are we
introducing bottlenecks

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.170
into this process of
producing knowledge?

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:30.220
So I want to walk you through,
in the next few slides,

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.650
how we apply some
of these principles

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:33.890
and how we design for them.

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.557
And then, what I want
to do is come back

00:10:35.557 --> 00:10:36.650
and make it more real.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.220
So we'll talk kind
of in an abstract way

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.220
about those principles
over the next few slides.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:43.870
And then we'll bring
it back, and we'll

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.760
talk through a real
use case and show you

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.640
how we've applied
those principles

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:52.430
in a real information pipeline.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:55.310
So let's start with
standardization, right?

00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:00.950
We want those components to
easily be swapped in and out,

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.990
but we want their
purpose to be obvious.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:06.552
That comes back, to me, to
readability and flexibility.

00:11:06.552 --> 00:11:07.260
Those two things.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:09.390
So let's start with readability.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:11.640
If we think about readability
from a code perspective,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.473
what does that mean?

00:11:12.473 --> 00:11:14.110
It means, fundamentally,
my code base

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.070
is more maintainable
because someone else

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.370
can come in and understand
what that software is intending

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:20.750
to do.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.240
We want our data model
to operate the same way.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.880
We want our consumers to
be able to come in, look

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.280
at one resource, get
familiar and comfortable

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:29.580
with that resource.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.120
And then, when they go
to use another resource,

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:35.180
not have to worry
about reconfiguring

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.120
their mental model.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:37.550
We want it to look similar.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:39.560
We want our resources
to look similar such

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.660
that they can jump
across those resources

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:43.190
with a minimal
amount of friction

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.490
and really combine data together
to produce new information

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.230
for the company.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:50.395
Standardization is
how we get there.

00:11:50.395 --> 00:11:51.770
And the way we've
standardized is

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:55.550
by adopting Google's resource
oriented design guidelines.

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:58.010
If you've ever used
the Google Cloud APIs,

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.550
this will feel familiar to you.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.870
This is a lot of the principles
that are applied in the Google

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:03.830
Cloud APIs.

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:07.360
And you'll see some
things show up here

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.160
in a few moments directly out
of those design guidelines.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:15.170
One thing that really
cemented for me

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.800
kind of putting
importance in API design

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.750
was Martin Nally's
talk last year

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.070
at Cloud Next called
Designing Quality APIs.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.820
It's a really good
watch if you're

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.620
interested in API design,
and why it's important,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.263
and how to go about it.

00:12:30.263 --> 00:12:32.680
It's a really good watch and
a really good place to start.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.750
Now, we talked
about readability.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:43.050
Standardization is helping
give us that readability.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.270
And it's helping
give us that ability

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.850
to swap in new components
as an engineering team.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.150
But what about flexibility?

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:56.280
If we're so rigid that we have
to change our APIs every time

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.830
a new attribute's introduced
into a business workflow

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:03.030
that maybe it's something
hyperspecific to that workflow,

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:04.980
that's going to be
retooling for us-- that's

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:08.010
going to be potentially
retooling for our consumers

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.050
by way of a breaking
change to or APIs,

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:13.140
or a new version of an API that
they have to rapidly adopt,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:13.770
right?

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.100
It's going to cause churn.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:18.030
It's going to potentially
cause downtime and loss

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.870
of productivity.

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:21.960
So what we've done is
we've adopted a concept

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:26.640
from the resource oriented
design guidelines.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.800
With these guidelines, we
try to not be dogmatic.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:29.920
We try to be pragmatic.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:33.960
So we'll start with the resource
oriented design guidelines, use

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:34.693
that as our base.

00:13:34.693 --> 00:13:37.110
And then, if there's something
that maybe makes more sense

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.720
because we have more context
about our development

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:43.440
workflow or our customer's
workflows, we'll adapt it.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:44.970
And we'll make it fit that.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:49.890
But we use the resource oriented
design guidelines as our base.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:52.240
Resource annotations
are an example of that.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:55.332
There's a concept in resource
oriented design called labels.

00:13:55.332 --> 00:13:57.040
And if you've used
the Google Cloud APIs,

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.373
again, you've seen this concept.

00:13:58.373 --> 00:14:02.220
Or really, any cloud API, you've
seen this concept in some way.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:06.320
It's essentially bring
your own context, right?

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.680
I don't have to tell
Google Cloud, hey,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.080
can you add this attribute
to the Compute Engine API

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.400
so that I can capture it?

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:14.570
I can add that.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:15.710
And it's specific to me.

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:17.390
And it's relevant to me.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:18.830
It's understandable to me.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:23.360
We've adopted that same
principle with our API design.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.540
We've got a lot of workflows
internally that spin up

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:28.010
and spin down quickly, right?

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.750
We want to be that base.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.830
We don't want to have
to change our APIs

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:37.100
and introduced downtime
with every change

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.780
to the business workflow.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:40.983
So this enables that
bring your own context.

00:14:40.983 --> 00:14:43.400
An example here is that this
is really just key value pair

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:44.150
data, right?

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:46.340
So we've got some
base schema that

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:52.040
is giving you a definition of
the core enterprise attributes

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.630
for that particular resource.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.338
And then, you've got
annotations that you

00:14:55.338 --> 00:14:57.080
can apply on top
of that resource

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.170
to bring your own context.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:02.090
An example would be if I want
to label a particular seed

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.460
as being part of a
drought initiative,

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:08.790
I could add that annotation
by way of a key value pair.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.820
And in some cases,
we do have controlled

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:13.250
vocabulary implemented over
some of those annotations.

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.680
But mostly, it's free form.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:21.290
It's meant to be a piece of
flexibility for our consumers.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:23.390
Another way that we
enable flexibility

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.570
is through this concept
of resource sets.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.010
So we've got
resource collections

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:31.040
that we expose as APIs.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.800
And then, I think
what we've seen

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.550
a lot of is that people
like their things,

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:37.250
and they like their
groups of things.

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:41.420
They want to bring context by
way of taxonomically organizing

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.850
that data in some way that's
meaningful and relevant

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:44.510
to them.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.130
Resource sets give us that.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:47.240
It's another resource.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:50.770
It's structured as a
resource standard API,

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:54.140
but it gives you the ability
to group a particular resource

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:56.090
by way of filter values, right?

00:15:56.090 --> 00:16:01.340
So let's say I wanted to keep
a list of all the products

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.230
that are moving through
the pipeline for 2019.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:07.160
If I have a product resource
and a product sets resource,

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:07.750
I can do that.

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:10.160
I can provide filter parameters
and I can either manually

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.010
update that thing,
or we're moving more

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:18.410
towards this concept of dynamic
sets, which is basically filter

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:20.450
parameters with context, right?

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:21.830
So give it a name.

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:22.550
Name that thing.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.130
And then, it can be reused
across the organization.

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:27.080
It's a way to
deduplicate that data,

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:30.860
but also give our consumers some
control and some flexibility

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.470
in the management of that data.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.240
The other side here
is that we don't

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:42.520
want to impose trade-offs
on our consumers

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:43.660
where we don't have to.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.390
We want to push the ability
to make trade-offs back

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.430
into their control.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.480
There are a couple of
ways that we do that.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:55.240
So I've talked in generality
about resource APIs, right?

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:58.800
Resource just being a
particular data set.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.270
We provide multiple
modes of access

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.860
to each of those data sets.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:05.349
One is gRPC.

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:10.210
So we've wholesale adopted
gRPC across our platform

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:12.790
as a mode of access
for each data set.

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:14.349
We've also enabled REST.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:17.109
We see definitely more
usage on the REST side

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:20.013
from less technical consumers.

00:17:20.013 --> 00:17:21.180
People like data scientists.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.200
People that are just that aren't
programmers but are trying

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:25.617
to get their job done and need
to interact with these APIs

00:17:25.617 --> 00:17:26.925
to access these data sets.

00:17:26.925 --> 00:17:29.800
REST is going to work with
more out of the box tooling

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:33.340
and just be an
easier experience,

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.740
but gRPC is going to give
you that performance.

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:39.880
We've done some head-to-head
benchmarks of REST versus gRPC

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:43.300
and there's definitely
noticeable performance gain

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:44.380
there with gRPC.

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.767
But as a consumer,
as a data scientist,

00:17:47.767 --> 00:17:49.600
I don't want to have a
high barrier to entry

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:50.725
for accessing these things.

00:17:50.725 --> 00:17:54.530
So we provide both of
these modes of access.

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:58.070
The kicker here is that if
I want to string together

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:02.513
multiple resources to really
make something meaningful,

00:18:02.513 --> 00:18:03.930
that can be more
difficult, right?

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.133
I have to be somewhat
of a programmer

00:18:06.133 --> 00:18:07.050
to be able to do that.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.900
To call these APIs, do some
sort of transformation,

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:14.250
pipe that into another API,
do transformation off of that.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:15.750
How do we make it
easy for consumers

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.060
to join this data together?

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.950
That's where GraphQL
comes in for us.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:23.250
So we've got a
GraphQL server that,

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:27.800
again, can be used by any
of our consumer personas--

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.570
bench scientists,
data scientists,

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.590
enterprise application--

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:36.480
that can use this GraphQL
server, which in turn calls

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:38.040
our gRPC APIs, right?

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.040
So we get the performance
of calling gRPC APIs,

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.990
but we can do a
prescriptive join

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.320
of that data for the consumers.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:50.690
This gives, again, the consumer
the ability to trade off

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:52.070
who's doing the join, right?

00:18:52.070 --> 00:18:54.380
If they have more knowledge
potentially about a better

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:58.322
way to pre-filter data and join
that with another data set,

00:18:58.322 --> 00:18:59.030
they can do that.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:01.220
They can do that by
way of gRPC or REST.

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.530
They have that ability.

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:06.228
If they don't want to join
that data together manually,

00:19:06.228 --> 00:19:07.020
they don't have to.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.190
They can call our
GraphQL API and we'll

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:11.150
do that join for them.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:16.120
Both of those are synchronous
modes of access, right?

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:18.988
Request response
for our consumers.

00:19:18.988 --> 00:19:20.530
The third pattern
here that we follow

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:24.070
is we've adopted Cloud
Native Computing Foundations

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:27.220
CloudEvents spec to publish
an event stream for each

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:28.750
of our resources.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:31.600
That event stream
notifies on standard

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:34.510
create, update, delete
operations, right?

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.430
It tells and give
our consumers a hook

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:42.580
into our ecosystem to react
asynchronously to goings

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.730
on in our ecosystem.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.320
This, again, gives
our consumers a way

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:51.640
to decide what model fits
their particular workflow.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.740
Whether that's an
enterprise application that

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:57.460
wants to react as new enterprise
state elements are created,

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:00.070
or a data scientist that
is really just trying

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.980
to get these data
sets combined together

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:06.970
and move on with their lives
so they can focus on their job,

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.722
their core responsibilities.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:16.320
That's heavily focused on
the consumer side, right?

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:18.540
As an engineering
team, we also try

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.600
to take that idea
of standardization

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.330
and use that as our
indirection to the back end.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:26.490
And that gives us the
ability to use data structure

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.060
as a competitive advantage.

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:29.760
So again, I mentioned
that we've got

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.530
40 plus resource collections.

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:33.660
They're all different
shapes and sizes.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:38.670
We've got data sets like our
pedigree ancestry data set that

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.920
tracks seed lineage, right?

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:43.590
If we think about
that corn kernel.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:44.370
We plant that.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:45.780
We pollinate it in some way.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.440
We harvest new seed.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.030
And those seeds are linked
together genetically, right?

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:51.520
One came from the other.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:54.810
And we do that over the
course of years, sometimes

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:58.800
multiple times a year for
that particular line of seed.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:00.000
That produces a lot of data.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.700
And that data is naturally
structured as a graph.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:07.950
And with that data set, what
you see on the right hand side

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:11.850
here of the slide is an
image that my colleague, Tim

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:15.480
Williamson, tweeted a
while back that is actually

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:20.160
a representation of our
ancestry data set for corn.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:23.910
Each of those stars in
this galaxy are corn seeds,

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:27.870
and the links between them
are genetic relationships.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.820
These seeds came from
these seeds, right?

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.430
It gives you a sense of
the scale of that data set.

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:33.420
This is just corn.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:39.865
Now, we work in a lot
more crops than just corn.

00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:41.740
If this is interesting
to you, I would highly

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.380
encourage that you go out
and watch Tim's presentation

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:47.290
from GraphConnect
a couple years ago.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:51.310
He goes more in-depth in how
we've modeled data in neo4j

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:53.440
for that pedigree data.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:55.690
But the point here
is that we can't

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:58.300
specialize from an engineering
perspective, right?

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.280
We have that indirection.

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:01.240
None of this is novel.

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:03.220
But that standardization
gives us a good way

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:04.900
to kind of plug and play.

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:07.150
We can start with a relational
database for a data set

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:08.980
like this if we
wanted to, and then

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:11.110
optimize the back end later.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:14.470
And with minimal friction,
or ideally no friction

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:17.470
for our consumers.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:18.970
The other side of
our data footprint

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:22.692
is Cloud Spanner has rapidly
become our primary database.

00:22:22.692 --> 00:22:24.400
So we're coming from
a legacy environment

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:26.890
with a large Oracle
instance that

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:30.160
was really good at its job for
what we needed at the time.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.102
As we've moved to more
of a microservice model,

00:22:33.102 --> 00:22:35.560
something like Cloud Spanner
has actually become very, very

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:36.370
useful for us.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:39.700
We've made smaller, thinner,
more well-defined resources

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:43.030
so we're not trying to
facilitate seven table joins

00:22:43.030 --> 00:22:44.345
to get data back.

00:22:44.345 --> 00:22:46.720
Cloud Spanner has been a really
good fit as well because,

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:50.210
again, different data sets of
different shapes and sizes.

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:52.210
Size being the key word in that.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.680
We have a few data sets
that are very, very large.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:58.180
And those are the data sets
that we'll actually talk through

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:01.420
in our real life example.

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:04.030
To give you a sense of the
scale of our Spanner footprint,

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:05.830
right now we're at
about 17 terabytes

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:07.995
across our two environments.

00:23:07.995 --> 00:23:11.080
About 10 terabytes in our
production environment.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.810
And the other seven in our
non-production environment.

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.480
And our instance size floats
between 10 and 50 nodes.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:17.985
Now that can get expensive.

00:23:17.985 --> 00:23:19.360
But we'll talk a
little bit later

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.570
about how we try to control
that cost without sacrificing

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:25.280
performance with
Spanner specifically.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:30.380
So we've talked a lot
about getting data out

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.290
of our platform.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.530
Let's talk about how we
get data into our platform.

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:36.860
Our most basic pattern for
getting data into our platform

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:40.340
is essentially a data generator
calling a resource API

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:42.170
by way of gRPC arrest, right?

00:23:42.170 --> 00:23:44.425
Again, nothing novel, but
there are some things,

00:23:44.425 --> 00:23:46.550
some characteristics about
that particular workflow

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:47.940
that are important.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:49.790
One, it pushes
resource validation

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:51.200
closer to the data generator.

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:53.540
So if the data generator
gives us bad data,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:54.830
that is synchronous.

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:56.570
We can immediately
reject that data

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.670
and give that data
generator context

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:01.160
as to why that data is invalid.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:05.450
It keeps muck out of our
enterprise systems, right?

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:07.880
And it also prevents us
as an engineering team

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:13.730
from having to get in the muck
if that bad data is generated.

00:24:13.730 --> 00:24:16.370
The other characteristic is that
the consistency model follows

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.350
that of the resource API.

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:20.600
So again, Cloud
Spanner is essentially

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:23.570
our primary database
at this point.

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:25.310
With a global
consistency model, we

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:30.440
can provide that same
capability to our consumers.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.600
The flip side of that,
the trade-off here,

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.450
is that the data generator is
sensitive to the performance

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:36.830
of the resource API.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:40.100
If our resource
API is slow and we

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:44.750
don't have alarm bells
sounding from our KPIs,

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:46.640
that data generator
is going to slow down,

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:48.580
or they're going to have to
deal with that in some way.

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:50.413
They're going to have
to engineer complexity

00:24:50.413 --> 00:24:52.470
into their system
to accommodate that.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:55.640
So in cases where
either it's not feasible

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.760
for us to make something
hyperperformant

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:04.520
or cases where that write
is just high enough latency

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:06.440
that it impacts
the data generator

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:10.520
and slows their work down,
this pattern may not fit.

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:14.570
There's another pattern where--

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:18.363
that pattern works well
with kind of this new world

00:25:18.363 --> 00:25:20.780
that we're building where we're
building new workflows out

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:24.250
in the Cloud and
engineering systems

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:27.080
to bring us into
a new landscape.

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:31.670
Another write
pattern that we have,

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:33.740
though, is we do have,
again, large legacy

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:37.130
systems that have collected
data over the course of 20

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.960
to 30 years.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:40.248
That data is still important.

00:25:40.248 --> 00:25:42.290
We still use that data to
make a lot of decisions

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:46.730
in our business because we want
to look at that data over time.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:50.090
The way we get our legacy data
into our systems in most cases

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.490
is through a product
that we have internally

00:25:52.490 --> 00:25:54.380
called the Enterprise Data Hub.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:57.710
What the Enterprise Data Hub
is a set of replicated Kafka

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:00.320
clusters that is managed
by a central engineering

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.500
team within our company.

00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:06.770
That product takes care of
bidirectional replication

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:08.770
between our different
cloud environments, right?

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:11.110
So we have a Kafka
cluster on prem.

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:13.030
We have a Kafka cluster in AWS.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:15.070
We have a Kafka cluster in GCP.

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:18.130
And Data Hub takes care of
moving that data in and out

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:20.590
of those environments for us.

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:22.000
On the front end
of that, we have

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:25.164
change data capture mechanisms.

00:26:25.164 --> 00:26:28.630
Informatica data replicator
in the case of SAP,

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:32.080
and Oracle GoldenGate in the
case of our Oracle environment.

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:34.388
Those are pushing change
capture records out.

00:26:34.388 --> 00:26:36.430
Those change capture
records are making their way

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:38.880
to Golang Kafka consumers
that we have running out

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.170
in Google Cloud, and pushing--

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:43.810
again, still pushing things
into our resource API.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:47.320
So we're dogfooding
those resource APIs, even

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:48.110
in this context.

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:51.250
We're not ever making
direct rights around the API

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:55.580
to our databases, which, again,
one hardens our workflows,

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:56.080
right?

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:57.440
If we're dogfooding
it, we're going

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:58.960
to harden that
workflow much quicker

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:03.490
than waiting for our
consumers to find issues.

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:06.430
And, too, it's given us
some of the same benefit.

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:10.930
We can leverage those
flexibility concepts as well

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:16.312
and adapt our
workflows much quicker.

00:27:16.312 --> 00:27:17.770
One thing I would
stress here, too,

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:20.830
is that if you're interested
in learning more about how

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.370
our data hub
environment is set up,

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:26.060
a former colleague, Bob
Lehman, speaks of that approach

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:27.520
in Kafka Summit last year.

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:29.950
This is really another
really good watch

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:34.030
that goes more in-depth
into our data hub setup.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:40.310
So that particular
change pattern

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:42.950
is generally lightweight
events, right?

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:47.720
The change capture records
are maybe 10, 15 columns.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:50.210
Not big.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:54.090
And a lot of those can be
kind of treated in isolation.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:57.750
However, we have a
set of workflows,

00:27:57.750 --> 00:28:00.030
particularly in
the genomics world

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:04.373
where we've got a lot of data
that comes from a single event.

00:28:04.373 --> 00:28:05.790
So we have a single
change capture

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.000
record that comes across.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.820
That single change
record can lead

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:16.770
to creation of thousands
of resources, all of which

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.780
are fairly heavy weight.

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:22.470
There's some meat to each of
those individual resources.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:26.280
To accommodate that
workflow, Kafka really

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:29.880
doesn't encourage you to
put super large messages

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:32.483
on a topic in a partition.

00:28:32.483 --> 00:28:34.650
So to accommodate that
workflow, we still stream out

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:36.402
that lightweight event.

00:28:36.402 --> 00:28:37.860
We transform that
lightweight event

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:40.560
by joining it back
to the legacy system

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:44.370
and exchanging that
lightweight event for n

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:47.390
number of large resources.

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:50.630
We take each of those resources
and we sync them as objects

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:53.130
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:55.428
When we do that, we rely
on bucket notifications

00:28:55.428 --> 00:28:56.970
to publish out the
other side, right?

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:59.472
That's our change
capture mechanism

00:28:59.472 --> 00:29:00.930
on the other side
of Cloud storage.

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:02.970
So that bucket
notification fires.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:06.660
We do, in some cases, a little
more manipulation or massaging

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:07.590
to that data.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:10.020
And then, again, write it
in through the resource API.

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:13.170
So a similar approach,
but we're accommodating

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:15.880
that large transformation that
has to happen in the middle

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:16.380
there.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:20.840
And we're doing that
with GCS and PubSub.

00:29:24.858 --> 00:29:27.400
One of the things that we get
out of this particular workflow

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:29.590
is that with our
Kafka workflows,

00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:32.650
we can't arbitrarily scale
our consumption, right?

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:35.260
Scaling of a Kafka consumer
is bound by the number

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:36.670
of partitions that you have.

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:40.030
And repartitioning is generally
not a pleasant exercise.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:42.490
PubSub gives us the ability
to arbitrarily scale here.

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:47.020
We have a single event that is
infrequent that comes across,

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:50.990
and we exchange that for a
larger amount of information.

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:52.750
And then, we want to
parallelize really

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:56.710
wide to consume that information
into our resource APIs

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:58.120
as rapidly as possible.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:00.050
PubSub gives us that ability.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:03.910
We can scale out much, much
wider with a button push.

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:06.820
Essentially with a change in
a Kubernetes deployment size,

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:09.475
a replica count.

00:30:09.475 --> 00:30:11.350
So that's one of the
benefits that we get out

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:13.930
of this particular
workflow as well.

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:21.110
We don't always have control
of our legacy system.

00:30:21.110 --> 00:30:24.140
And by control, I mean the
ability to go back and exchange

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:25.980
information with it directly.

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.070
An example of that
for us is SAP.

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:32.150
So we don't ever touch our SAP
environment by querying it.

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:35.720
But we do have to change data
capture that's generated out

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:38.670
of our SAP system.

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:40.530
To accommodate
that, we've actually

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:43.680
paired Kafka in those
change capture records

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:45.030
with Spanner and data flow.

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.870
So this kind of more of
the traditional replicate

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:51.540
the data as is we replicate
as is into the Spanner.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:54.340
So you'll see tables
that look like SAP tables

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:56.040
in Cloud Spanner.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:31:00.780
And then, we use Cloud Dataflow
to kind of give us this concept

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.700
of a materialized view, right?

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:06.210
We've got Cloud Dataflow
running periodically

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:10.620
as a scheduled job, taking
data out of Cloud Spanner,

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:13.710
making it into the format
that we want to make it into

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:17.490
to make it a more descriptive
resource for our consumers,

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:19.260
storing that data
back in Cloud Spanner,

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:22.122
and then our resource
APIs built on top of that.

00:31:22.122 --> 00:31:23.580
So it's essentially
a cache, right?

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:27.810
One of the things
that we get out

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:30.450
of using Cloud
Dataflow here-- we're

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:33.060
heavily using Cloud
Dataflow Java.

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:36.000
But one of things we get out
of that is testable business

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:36.870
logic, right?

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:38.910
We can test those
data transformations

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:40.180
that we're doing.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:44.100
Whereas, if we're traditionally
building 1,000 line SQL

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:47.080
statement to build
a materialized view,

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:50.430
you get testing that is
really hard, and error prone,

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:52.170
and all these other
things that make

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:55.170
it really difficult
to ensure we're not

00:31:55.170 --> 00:31:56.520
introducing breaking changes.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:58.260
With Dataflow and with
Java, obviously we

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:00.780
can unit test in a
similar way that we would

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:03.606
any of our other software.

00:32:03.606 --> 00:32:06.390
Another aspect of data
flow that becomes important

00:32:06.390 --> 00:32:09.780
here is that we can scale that.

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:13.350
As our data set size scales, we
can increase our worker count.

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:14.950
Again, it's a button push.

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:19.140
So that gives us the ability
to ingest a lot more data

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:22.170
if need be without a lot
of operational overhead

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:24.247
or having to retool
our environment.

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:28.730
The trade-off here
with this workflow

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:31.767
is that this is
not near real time.

00:32:31.767 --> 00:32:34.350
These other workflows that we've
looked at are near real time.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.380
Moments after that data is
written to the legacy system,

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:40.560
it'll be available
in our resource APIs

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:43.170
for consumption
by our consumers.

00:32:43.170 --> 00:32:43.670
This is not.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:44.540
This is scheduled.

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:47.750
So this is every few hours,
we run these transformations.

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:52.542
We can squeeze that down and
make it shorter frequency.

00:32:52.542 --> 00:32:54.000
We haven't had the
need to do that.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:33:00.800
But it does suffer from
this particular aspect.

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:02.750
What you see on the right
is an example of one

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:03.917
of our simple Dataflow jobs.

00:33:03.917 --> 00:33:06.020
So when I talk
about testability,

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:09.650
this is kind of showing
this is a simple job.

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:11.636
Testing these things
as it is important.

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:18.150
So we talked a little
bit earlier about

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:20.430
how it's important for us
to have key performance

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:21.840
indicators.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:24.420
A view of our
factory floor, right?

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:27.580
We don't want to ever stop
that production of knowledge.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:29.982
We want that to
be available 24/7.

00:33:29.982 --> 00:33:31.440
So now, I want to
talk a little bit

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:33.540
about some of the reliability
engineering aspects

00:33:33.540 --> 00:33:36.360
that we've introduced
into our stack.

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.510
The most basic, but also
one of the most useful,

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:44.580
has been instrumenting our APIs
to capture access log data, API

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:47.070
request data.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:50.950
This captures essentially
the who, the what, the when,

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:53.550
and the how long for
each of the API requests

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:55.560
that coming into our platform.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:34:00.210
This gives us a sense of how
those requests or those APIs

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:02.310
are operating for
our consumers, right?

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:06.000
If it's something slow,
we surface this data

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.520
through BigQuery
written through PubSub.

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:12.927
So each API hands off
the request to PubSub.

00:34:12.927 --> 00:34:15.010
We've got a process that
syncs that into BigQuery.

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:17.070
And then, we surface it
for our engineering teams

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:18.870
using Data Studio.

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:21.300
So kind of gives you that
end to end experience,

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:24.510
and gives our engineering team
a view of that factory floor,

00:34:24.510 --> 00:34:27.820
but a of their specific
segment of that factory floor.

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:32.370
Another aspect.

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:37.980
You've seen how many of the
Ingress patterns rely on Kafka.

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:40.199
What we've done is
create, essentially,

00:34:40.199 --> 00:34:42.210
a monitor for Kafka.

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:43.770
That's not novel, right?

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:48.179
But what we did in this
case is use Kubernetes CRDs,

00:34:48.179 --> 00:34:50.790
custom resource definitions,
to allow our engineering team

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:54.179
to define their monitoring
requirements for their Kafka

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:56.530
consumers at deployment time.

00:34:56.530 --> 00:34:59.310
So instead of having to
deploy my Kafka consumer

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:01.260
and then go somewhere
else to say here's

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:03.510
how I should be
alerted for this thing,

00:35:03.510 --> 00:35:06.060
you can push that information
close to the deployment.

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:08.910
This is a place where,
to me, Kubernetes really,

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:09.720
really shines.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:13.620
This extensibility allows us
to describe our deployment

00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:16.560
workflow in the
terms that we use,

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:19.910
and make it discoverable
for our engineers

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:22.290
all through the surface that
they're interacting with

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:26.180
to do their application
development daily.

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:27.290
That alerts the slack.

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:31.070
Push the messages close to
where the engineers are.

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.980
But it's a set of really
simple go processes

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:37.190
that are watching for that
custom resource definition

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:39.210
to change.

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:41.990
And last but not least, we've
talked a lot about Spanner.

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:44.570
And I mentioned that if
we're running a large Spanner

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:47.077
instance, that
can get expensive.

00:35:47.077 --> 00:35:49.160
We also don't want to run
a small Spanner instance

00:35:49.160 --> 00:35:52.580
and have that seasonal
workload spike on us

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:58.240
all of a sudden and
our API performance

00:35:58.240 --> 00:35:59.650
goes down the drain, right?

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.810
We want stability, but we
also want cost control.

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:04.780
So we've written a simple
autoscaler component

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:07.110
for Cloud Spanner that
runs in Kubernetes, uses

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:10.900
the Stackdriver API to watch for
CPU utilization numbers, right?

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:14.410
It watches that time series
and scales Cloud Spanner

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:19.160
up and down using the
Spanner API as needed.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:22.240
We've seen this drastically
reduce our Spanner bill

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:26.080
because we were
overallocating before

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:28.820
to guard against
poor performance

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:31.060
if we saturated the instance.

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:33.280
But we've seen now
that CPU utilization

00:36:33.280 --> 00:36:38.050
with this autoscaler component
hover between 30% and 50% usage

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.360
utilization.

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:42.820
So we want to be using as much
of that capacity as we can.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:45.490
We can't put those
CPU cycles in the bank

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:46.880
and save them for later.

00:36:46.880 --> 00:36:51.230
We're getting what we pay
for at that point in time.

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:54.310
This has been a big addition
in terms of operations.

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:57.280
Again, hands off for
our engineering team.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.043
This autoscaler can take action.

00:36:59.043 --> 00:37:01.210
There's a little bit of
risk in that the Stackdriver

00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:02.450
API is a minute behind.

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:05.150
So if we see a
real sudden burst,

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:07.690
we may see a few
failed requests.

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:12.670
But overall, over time, it
helps keep our SLOs in check.

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:16.030
So let's make this real.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:22.630
Let's talk a little bit
about one of the concepts

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:24.400
that we've implemented
on our platform.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:25.960
This information
pipeline that we've

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:29.290
implemented on our platform over
the course of the last year.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:31.680
What we're doing is we're
doing a process called

00:37:31.680 --> 00:37:33.380
genotype inference.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:37.400
So think about that corn
seed again, that kernel.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:43.230
Again, that is each kernel
is dense in information.

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:45.120
What we want is
we want a picture,

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:49.380
a window into the information
inside that kernel.

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.020
Generate markers give
us that information

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:55.110
about the genome of that
particular corn seed, right?

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:58.260
So genome being all the
different nucleotide bases

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:02.710
that compose that
kernel genetically.

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:04.510
Genetic marker giving
some measurement

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:06.880
at particular locations
on the genome.

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:10.360
And those locations are picked
very strategically, right?

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:12.730
We're looking for things
that ultimately we

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:15.400
know this marker or
these set of markers

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:19.720
lead to this kernel being
white or yellow, right?

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:22.540
Things that we can observe
that have meaning and value

00:38:22.540 --> 00:38:25.620
for our customers.

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:27.640
The way we make
those observations--

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:31.260
we have two different
levels of resolution.

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:33.720
In red, you'll see low
density resolution.

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:36.150
What that is a smaller
number of genetic markers

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:40.360
that we capture spread
across the genome.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:43.470
In yellow, you'll see
high density observations.

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:44.720
That's a lot more markers.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:47.630
It's awful lot more
expensive to collect, right?

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:49.880
So these are things where
we're actually sending seeds

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:54.140
to the lab, and we're
running experiments

00:38:54.140 --> 00:38:58.840
to get a window into the
genome of these corn seeds.

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:02.678
Now, if we want to capture
low density observations,

00:39:02.678 --> 00:39:03.220
it's cheaper.

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:04.165
It's easier.

00:39:04.165 --> 00:39:07.000
It can be done more quickly.

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:08.840
But it still costs
a lot of money.

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:10.990
Still costs money, right?

00:39:10.990 --> 00:39:13.840
And it's not just the money
of running that experiment.

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:15.580
It's all the shipping
costs, and all

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:19.780
that comes into play to
move seed to actually make

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:21.820
those measurements.

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:25.300
What we've done is we've
leveraged mathematics,

00:39:25.300 --> 00:39:28.900
essentially, and programming,
and these technologies

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:34.120
that you've seen me talk about
to infer these genotypes, which

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:35.740
is at a much cheaper cost.

00:39:35.740 --> 00:39:38.710
We can basically take the data
that we've measured-- those two

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:41.690
different levels of resolution--
and we can fill in the gaps.

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:44.260
We can up sample the
low density resolution

00:39:44.260 --> 00:39:47.320
to a high density
resolution-- high resolution.

00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:49.423
And we can fill in
those middle areas

00:39:49.423 --> 00:39:51.340
where maybe we didn't
collect any data at all,

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:55.030
but we know genetically, the
seed came from the seed came

00:39:55.030 --> 00:39:56.020
from this seed.

00:39:56.020 --> 00:40:00.910
And genetics gives us certain
rules that we can put into play

00:40:00.910 --> 00:40:04.540
and make estimations
about what the genome

00:40:04.540 --> 00:40:06.790
of that particular
seed in the middle,

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:08.890
of which we made no
direct observations,

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:10.400
actually looks like.

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:14.440
We get probabilistic value that
allows, again, our consumers

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:17.020
to make the choice
of how confident they

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:20.890
want to be about that
picture of the genome, right?

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:23.920
Again, allowing the consumer
to make more decisions.

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:29.510
To give you a sense
of how much data

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:32.960
we've generated with this
information pipeline, what

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:36.210
you see, again, in yellow is
the high density observation.

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:40.850
So we collected a lot of
information, a lot of markers

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:45.503
on a smaller number of seeds.

00:40:45.503 --> 00:40:46.920
With the low density
observations,

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:50.682
we've collected a
lot fewer marker.

00:40:50.682 --> 00:40:52.140
We've observed a
lot fewer markers,

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:56.130
but we've done it on a
larger number of seeds.

00:40:56.130 --> 00:40:59.772
The blue is the area
that we've filled in.

00:40:59.772 --> 00:41:01.480
I talked to a colleague
this morning just

00:41:01.480 --> 00:41:03.940
to make sure that my number
was right on that number--

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:05.950
or on that blue area.

00:41:05.950 --> 00:41:08.080
We're talking about
197 billion genotypes

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:14.840
that we were able to generate
and infer from those data sets.

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:17.110
This something that was
previously unachievable,

00:41:17.110 --> 00:41:19.710
and we've done it at
a much lower cost.

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:24.160
And we've done it this way.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:26.420
So let's walk through
this diagram real quick.

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:31.480
This is architecture for this
genotype inference pipeline.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:33.610
Let's talk about some
of those principles

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:36.620
that we laid out earlier.

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:39.550
So if you go left to right,
starting from the bottom,

00:41:39.550 --> 00:41:41.350
we've got a lab
workflow, a lab system

00:41:41.350 --> 00:41:46.450
that's pushing data in to our
observed genotypes resource.

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:48.370
That's backed by Cloud Spanner.

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.680
All of that data is
in Cloud Spanner.

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:52.730
From there, you've
got a cloud event.

00:41:52.730 --> 00:41:54.517
You get that
asynchronous notification

00:41:54.517 --> 00:41:55.600
that new data has arrived.

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:01.260
There's a component called
the orchestrator that's

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:03.420
listening for those
events, and then

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:05.730
scheduling these inference jobs.

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:08.190
And it's doing
that on Kubernetes

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:13.070
by way of GKE as
PubSub messages that

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:18.800
get distributed across multiple
regions, multiple Kubernetes

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:21.190
clusters in multiple regions.

00:42:21.190 --> 00:42:23.870
Then, those pods, their
sole purpose in life

00:42:23.870 --> 00:42:26.030
is to do that
calculation for us.

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:29.660
To do that calculation,
to infer these genotypes,

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:32.750
they'll reach back in
to our resource APIs

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:35.720
to grab those
observed genotypes.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:38.360
When they complete
their purpose,

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:42.770
they'll write that new data
set out as an inferred genotype

00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:44.960
into our inferred
genotype resource.

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:47.660
One interesting thing about
that, one of the Spanner

00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:51.410
talks that we were in
earlier this morning,

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:55.920
they talked about using
Spanner for GCS metadata.

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:57.510
Similar approach
here, we actually

00:42:57.510 --> 00:42:59.350
use we stored the
metadata for these data

00:42:59.350 --> 00:43:02.910
sets in Spanner so that we
can more rapidly search over.

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:05.340
It but the bulk of the
data, the payload for that

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.800
is stored in GCS
because it is large.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.540
We're talking 85,000 genotypes
for a single seed, right?

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:14.370
And then, we're talking
millions of seeds

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:17.230
that we're inferring
these genotypes for.

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:20.550
So that that data
set grows rapidly.

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:22.310
A few interesting
characteristics

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:24.420
to facilitate this workflow.

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:29.040
The workers actually
run on preemptable VMs.

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:31.980
So that's actually been
a huge mechanism for us

00:43:31.980 --> 00:43:33.840
to control costs
in this process.

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:35.400
Preemptible VMs you
get at a fraction

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:38.460
of the cost of a
normal VM, but you

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:40.470
get that resource--
those compute resources

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:42.390
for a limited amount of time.

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:45.150
To engineer for
resilience to preemption,

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:47.740
we've done a couple of things.

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:48.700
One is checkpointing.

00:43:48.700 --> 00:43:51.540
So we checkpoint
intermediate state out to GCS

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:54.210
as these workers run so that
if they do get preempted,

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:56.430
they can pick back up
from where they left off.

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:59.283
These jobs can be long running
on the order of minutes

00:43:59.283 --> 00:44:01.200
at times just depending
on how much data there

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:06.240
is for a particular seed.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:09.810
And with that in mind, with
that long running characteristic

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:14.970
in mind, we've implemented a job
lock using Cloud Memorystore.

00:44:14.970 --> 00:44:17.370
So PubSub likes to
try to re deliver

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:20.310
these messages
sometimes before we're

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:22.537
ready to be done
with them, right?

00:44:22.537 --> 00:44:23.620
So we've implemented them.

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:26.010
We've used Memorystore
as a simple job lock

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:30.423
where we write some metadata to
Memorystore as the job starts.

00:44:30.423 --> 00:44:32.340
And then, if we get
reader delivered a message

00:44:32.340 --> 00:44:34.200
from PubSub, we can
check and ensure

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:38.310
that we don't duplicate effort.

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:39.900
The last thing I
want to call out here

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:43.380
is that, again, this all comes
back to these resource APIs.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:45.150
We're reading from
these resource APIs,

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:47.230
and writing output to
these resource APIs.

00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:49.160
We're dog fooding all
of these resource APIs

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:51.220
that we're exposing
to our consumers.

00:44:51.220 --> 00:44:53.100
We see the same surface they do.

00:44:53.100 --> 00:44:55.920
So that forces us to
make sure that surface

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:57.270
is coherent and usable.

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:04.460
To give you a sense of how
much data we've generated,

00:45:04.460 --> 00:45:07.870
again, 197 billion genotypes.

00:45:07.870 --> 00:45:10.150
To backfill this,
the process itself is

00:45:10.150 --> 00:45:11.420
meant to be always on, right?

00:45:11.420 --> 00:45:16.030
As new data arrives,
we make new inferences.

00:45:16.030 --> 00:45:17.560
We had to backfill
this in some way.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:20.297
And we did that, again, by
dog fooding the process.

00:45:20.297 --> 00:45:22.630
At a single moment in time--
actually, during Cloud Next

00:45:22.630 --> 00:45:25.990
last year, we had a
Kubernetes cluster

00:45:25.990 --> 00:45:32.740
that was using 42,500 virtual
CPUs all in preemptable nodes

00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:33.510
at one time.

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:35.810
270 terabytes of memory.

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:38.500
And when we talk about
cost, we were doing this

00:45:38.500 --> 00:45:41.350
at $0.01 per CPU
hour, which means

00:45:41.350 --> 00:45:43.840
that as we scale
this process up,

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:45.550
our costs don't go
out of control, right?

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:47.138
This doesn't become prohibitive.

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:52.760
That's all I've got.

00:45:52.760 --> 00:45:54.560
That's my Twitter handle.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:57.170
We're always happy to talk about
what we do and how we do it.

